  "reason": "The source assertion 'LLMs with more parameters almost always make better predictions than LLMs with fewer parameters' uses the label 'makes' (defined as producing outputs) combined with the qualifier 'better'. The target relation 'outperforms' is defined as achieving superior results compared to another entity. This captures the exact semantic meaning of 'making better predictions' while preserving the directionality (more parameters -> outperforms fewer parameters). Other relations like 'causes' or 'enables' fail to capture the comparative superiority ('better') explicitly stated in the qualifier, and 'generates_predictions_faster_than' incorrectly shifts focus to speed rather than quality/accuracy."
